WHERE THE MIT CLAIM COMES FROM
------------------------------
That repository has no LICENSE file. Its README states, verbatim:

    License
    =======

    Code (AVR projects): GPL3.0.

    Code (STM32F projects): MIT license.

    Hardware: cc-by-sa-3.0

Every project vendored here — braids, clouds, elements, peaks, plaits,
rings, warps — is an STM32F project, so all of it falls on the MIT side of
that line. The AVR projects (Grids and Branches among them; grids/makefile
sets MCU_NAME = 328 and every Grids source file carries a GPL-3.0 header)
are not vendored. No hardware file is vendored either, so cc-by-sa-3.0 does
not apply to anything here.

The README is not the only evidence. All 276 files taken from those seven
projects carry the MIT permission notice in their own header, which is the
licence grant that actually travels with the code; so do 30 of the 32 files
of stmlib, the shared library they all build on. The full text is in
LICENSE-MIT beside this file. The two exceptions in stmlib are not Emilie
Gillet's work and are listed below.

    $ for f in $(find . -name '*.h' -o -name '*.cc'); do
          grep -q "Permission is hereby granted, free of charge" $f || echo $f
      done

